---
import Article from '../layouts/Article.astro';

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'SoftwareApplication',
    name: 'Remy',
    alternateName: 'Hey Remy',
    url: 'https://www.hey-remy.com/',
    applicationCategory: 'LifestyleApplication',
    operatingSystem: 'iOS',
    description:
      'Remy is the AI assistant for your life, not your job. It reads your Gmail and Google Calendar with your permission, catches what you would miss in school and life-admin emails, makes phone calls on your behalf, and handles errands - with every outward action held until you press and hold to approve.',
    offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD', description: 'Free during beta' },
    author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Hoot Ventures' },
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Remy', url: 'https://www.hey-remy.com/' },
    featureList: [
      'Reads Gmail and surfaces the one line that matters in long emails',
      'Heads-ups on deadlines, renewals, refills, and trips before they slip',
      'Makes phone calls on your behalf and returns a transcript',
      'Drafts and sends email, books calendar events, fills web forms - after press-and-hold approval',
      'Remembers people, decisions, and dates',
      'One chat, nothing to configure',
    ],
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Organization',
    name: 'Remy',
    url: 'https://www.hey-remy.com/',
    logo: 'https://www.hey-remy.com/remy-mark.png',
    founder: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Rohit Kapoor' },
    parentOrganization: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Hoot Ventures' },
    email: 'support@hootventures.ai',
  },
];
---

<Article
  title="What is Remy? The AI assistant for your life, not your job"
  description="Remy is an iPhone app: an AI assistant that reads your Gmail and calendar with your permission, catches what you'd miss, makes phone calls, and handles life admin - every action approved by you. Here is exactly what it does, who it's for, and who it isn't for."
  eyebrow="Plain answer"
  heading="What is Remy?"
  lede="The short version, then the details - including who should not use it."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <div class="answer">
    <p>
      <b>Remy is an AI assistant for your personal life.</b> It's an iPhone app. You sign in with
      Google, and Remy reads your Gmail and Google Calendar on your behalf. It catches the things
      you'd miss in long emails (the soccer registration deadline buried in the PTA newsletter),
      gives you a heads-up before renewals and refills slip, makes phone calls for you (the salon,
      the doctor's office), fills in forms, and remembers the people and dates that matter.
    </p>
    <p>
      <b>Nothing happens without you.</b> Every email, booking, and call is shown to you first.
      You press and hold to approve, or it doesn't happen.
    </p>
    <p>
      <b>It's not a work tool.</b> No meeting notes, tickets, or team features. The line is
      "the AI assistant for your life, not your job", and we mean it literally.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Best for</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <b>People whose inbox is full of life admin</b> - school newsletters, camp deposits,
      insurance, renewals, appointment confirmations - and who miss things because the important
      line is buried in paragraph four.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>People who put off phone calls.</b> Remy calls the salon, the pediatrician, the
      contractor; waits on hold; comes back with the appointment and a transcript.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>People who don't want to learn a system.</b> There's one chat. You text it like a
      friend, typos and all. There are no commands, boards, or settings to maintain.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>People who care where their data goes.</b> Remy is not in the business of building AI
      models. Your data is used to help you and for nothing else.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Not for</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <b>Work.</b> If you want your tasks auto-scheduled around meetings, use Motion or Reclaim.
      If you want meeting notes, use a note-taker. Remy doesn't do any of that.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>A shared family wall calendar.</b> Skylight and Cozi are built for a calendar everyone in
      the house looks at. Remy is one person's assistant; it reads your email and acts for you.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Android or desktop today.</b> Remy is iPhone-only during the beta.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Email accounts other than Google</b>, for now. Remy signs in with Google and reads Gmail
      and Google Calendar.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What Remy actually does</h2>
  <p>Real things it does today, in the order most people meet them:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <b>Reads the long emails so you don't have to.</b> A PTA newsletter arrives; Remy sends
      one line: "Nathan's soccer registration closes Friday. I pulled the form for you." The
      newsletter had nine other paragraphs. You didn't read them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Gives a heads-up while there's still time.</b> Your Lisbon flights come through; Remy
      remembers your passport expires five weeks before you fly and says so, with the renewal
      link.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Makes the call.</b> "Book my haircut with Dana, Saturday morning or Tuesday after 5."
      Remy calls the salon, books Saturday 10:30, adds it to your calendar, and shows you the
      transcript.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Drafts and sends email, with your OK.</b> The reply is written, shown to you, and sent
      only after you press and hold.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Fills in the form.</b> The car registration renewal on the county site, your plate
      already entered, ready for your OK.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Takes a whole project.</b> "Fix the leaking gutter" becomes a room where Remy asks three
      contractors for quotes with your photos, tracks the replies over days, and comes back only
      when the decision is yours: "Mike can do Thursday 8 AM for $450. Book him?"
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Remembers.</b> Who Dana is. That Mom's refills are ready Thursdays. That Sam's birthday
      needs a park permit. You don't re-explain your life every time.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How it works, in three steps</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><b>Install Remy</b> on your iPhone.</li>
    <li><b>Sign in with Google.</b> That connects Gmail and Google Calendar. Nothing to configure.</li>
    <li><b>Say hi.</b> From the first minute Remy is reading, and within the day it has a note for you.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>How it's different from ChatGPT, Gemini, and Siri</h2>
  <p>
    <b>ChatGPT</b> is brilliant when you bring it the problem. It doesn't sit on your inbox
    and notice the deadline on its own, and it doesn't call the salon. <b>Gemini</b> lives inside
    Google: it summarizes an email or adds an event when you ask, and its newer Spark agent can
    run ongoing tasks for paying subscribers; Google's own privacy notice says a subset of chats
    are reviewed by humans and kept up to three years, and asks you not to enter confidential
    information.
    <b>Siri</b> sets timers. Remy is the one that's already read the newsletter, already
    remembers the passport date, and comes to you with the one line, then does the thing when you
    approve. Longer comparisons live on the <a href="/blog">blog</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Privacy, in one paragraph</h2>
  <p>
    Remy reads your email and calendar to help you, and for nothing else. When it uses an AI
    model to think, that request is processed and not kept: the AI providers Remy uses are bound
    to zero-data-retention terms (the list, with each provider's policy, is at
    <a href="/ai-model-providers">/ai-model-providers</a>). Email and access tokens are
    encrypted at rest. You can delete everything from inside the app. We make money one way only:
    people paying for Remy. No ads, no selling data. The founder's full reasoning is in
    <a href="/why">Why I built Remy</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Price and availability</h2>
  <p>
    <b>Free during the beta.</b> iPhone only. Early access is open from this site. Paid plans will
    come; the business is people paying for Remy, not advertising.
  </p>

  <h2>Who makes it</h2>
  <p>
    Remy is built by Hoot Ventures, founded by Rohit Kapoor. He built it because he wanted an assistant his wife, his teenager, and his almost-80-year-old mom could use without
    configuring anything, and without handing their lives to a company that trains models on
    them. More in the <a href="/faq">FAQ</a>.
  </p>
</Article>
